  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1023 AM MDT Tue May 12 2026\n\nVery warm conditions will be the main story through Wed as the upper \nridge shifts to the east. Weak convection develops this afternoon \nover the Sierra Madres with a few sprinkles possible over the high \nterrain. Scattered CU can be expected today out west and over area \nmtns with light winds area-wide. A shortwave trough breaks down the \nridge on Wed, bringing some moisture and increasing clouds during \nthe day. A few virga showers are likely in the afternoon and evening \nacross northern areas with some gusty downdrafts possible. An \nisolated dry lightning strike cannot be ruled out in the Gila \nRegion/Sacs due to marginally unstable air. Very dry air holds below \nthe cloud layer, keeping rain chances near zero for the lowlands. \nThe mid-high clouds clear to the east by Thu AM.\n\nFrom Thu through the weekend, dry and breezy conditions will prevail \nunder SW/zonal flow aloft. Lee troughing develops each day along the \nSouthern High Plains with W-SW winds of about 10-20 mph in the \nafternoon. Impacts will be minimal with no blowing dust expected, \nalthough there will be some fire wx concerns due to the very low \nRHs. Thursday looks to be slightly breezier than the other days with \ngusts to 35 mph. Global ensembles are suggesting a deeper upper \ntrough moving into the region early next week, which would create \nstronger winds, but confidence is low. \n\nTemperatures peak on Wed with lowland highs in the mid-upper 90s \n(KELP has an 18% chance of hitting 100 for the first time this \nyear). Temps moderate slowly towards normal early next week.",
